{
  "assessment": "The source reports an experimental and simulation study of passive freeze-out of the Richtmyer-Meshkov instability (RMI) using engineered sub-surface voids. The key quantitative cluster is the comparison of jet tip velocity and mean interface velocity between baseline and suppression geometries, with a linear-theory model predicting jetting speed from shock impulse contributions. The source provides two regimes (baseline and suppression) with measured velocities, a fitted linear model with parameters, and fixed conditions. The extraction focuses on this cluster, encoding the measured velocities as readouts, the geometry and shock parameters as fixed conditions, and the linear RMI growth model as the mechanism. The case is authoring-ready because there is at least one numeric control (wavelength, amplitude), numeric readouts (jet tip velocity, mean interface velocity), a quantitative mechanism, and an objective source-grounded response within an explicit domain.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "J. Strucka, Passive Freeze-Out of the Richtmyer-Meshkov Instability, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/3wgy-sgkz",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/3wgy-sgkz",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2602.21375v1"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper reports the first experimental observation of passive freeze-out of the Richtmyer-Meshkov instability (RMI) in a low-pressure surrogate regime, using additively manufactured sub-surface voids in a sinusoidal target to convert a single shock into a sequence of weaker shocks. It compares baseline and suppression geometries via X-ray imaging and hydrodynamic simulations, assessing contributions from shock attenuation, temporal shaping, and spatial shaping. The selected quantitative cluster focuses on the measured jet tip and mean interface velocities for both geometries, and the linear-theory model predicting jetting speed from shock impulse contributions.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "rmi_linear_growth",
      "statement": "Linear Richtmyer-Meshkov growth rate: da/dt = (2*pi/lambda) * a(t0) * A * Delta_u, where a is perturbation amplitude, lambda is wavelength, A is Atwood number, and Delta_u is free surface velocity after shock compression. The ratio beta = (v_jet - v_mean)/v_mean = 2*pi*a(t0)*A/lambda depends only on initial geometry in the linear regime.",
      "equations": [
        "da(t)/dt = (2*pi/lambda) * a(t0) * A * Delta_u",
        "beta = (v_jet - v_mean)/v_mean = 2*pi*a(t0)*A/lambda"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["a", "lambda", "A", "Delta_u", "v_jet", "v_mean", "beta"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "rmi_coeff_2pi",
          "value": 6.283185307179586,
          "uncertainty": "Exact constant 2*pi, no uncertainty reported.",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Coefficient in linear RMI growth rate",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 204,
              "line_end": 204,
              "support": "Equation (RMI) gives da/dt = (2*pi/lambda) a(t0) A Delta_u, so the coefficient 2*pi appears explicitly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "atwood_number_A",
          "value": -1,
          "uncertainty": "Reported as approximately -1, no uncertainty given.",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Atwood number for gelatine-air interface",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 169,
              "line_end": 169,
              "support": "Atwood number A = (rho_air - rho_g)/(rho_air + rho_g) approx -1."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 253,
              "line_end": 253,
              "support": "Atwood number of the interface between gelatine and air is A = -1."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 200,
          "line_end": 206,
          "support": "Linear growth rate equation and beta ratio are defined and used to interpret attenuation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "temporal_shaping_model",
      "statement": "The total jetting speed from multiple shocks is modeled as a linear sum of contributions from each shock: (da/dt)|_shocks = (2*pi/lambda) * sum_n a(t_n) * A * Delta_u_n, where Delta_u_n = (I_n/I_tot)*Delta_u is the velocity imparted by each shock scaled by its impulse contribution. This model predicts jetting speed reduction from baseline to suppression.",
      "equations": [
        "(da/dt)|_shocks = (2*pi/lambda) * sum_n a(t_n) * A * Delta_u_n",
        "Delta_u_n = (I_n/I_tot) * Delta_u"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["a(t_n)", "lambda", "A", "Delta_u_n", "I_n", "I_tot", "Delta_u"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "shock1_impulse_I1",
          "value": 1.6,
          "uncertainty": "Reported as I_1 = 1.6 Pa*s, no uncertainty given.",
          "unit": "Pa*s",
          "role": "Impulse of first shock in suppression case",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 210,
              "line_end": 210,
              "support": "First shock at t1 approx 0.25 us with impulse I1 = 1.6 Pa*s."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "shock2_impulse_I2",
          "value": 19.4,
          "uncertainty": "Reported as I_2 = 19.4 Pa*s, no uncertainty given.",
          "unit": "Pa*s",
          "role": "Impulse of second shock in suppression case",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 210,
              "line_end": 210,
              "support": "Second shock at t2 approx 1.25 us with impulse I2 = 19.4 Pa*s."
            }
